;
; Handoff — carrier generation, OOK gating and self-measurement. M3, M13.
; design §10.1, §6.3, §9.8; development plan M3, M13.
;
; TWO STATE MACHINES ON ONE PAD, which is what lets M3 measure itself with no
; external hardware at all: the PIO input mux reads the pad independently of
; whichever peripheral is driving it, so the counter below sees the carrier the
; generator above is producing without a jumper, a scope, or a second board.
;

;
; carrier_out — one pad LEVEL and one pad DIRECTION per half-period.
;
; Each half-period is a SLOT of three state-machine cycles and two stream
; bits: the first bit is the pad level, the second is whether the pad is
; driven at all. A mark chip is slots (1,drive) (0,drive) (1,drive) ... ; a
; space chip is slots (x,release) — the pad is high-impedance for the whole
; chip, not driven low. design §9.8 has the numbers: a driven-low space pulls
; the wristband's own stage-1 bias to 0.28 V and C1/R6 then take ~17 ms to
; come back, against a 1 ms turnaround budget; a released pad sits at VREF
; and there is nothing to recover from.
;
; Three cycles rather than two because the half-period must be a whole number
; of system cycles: 150 MHz / (2 x 200 kHz) = 375 = 3 x 125, and the 40 kHz
; bring-up carrier gives 1875 = 3 x 625. Two cycles would need 187.5. config.h
; static-asserts the division.
;
; This is deliberately a bit stream rather than a counted toggle loop. A loop
; that pulls a chip and then toggles N times spends different numbers of cycles
; on the mark path and the space path unless every branch is hand-balanced, and
; an unbalanced branch shifts every chip after the first gated one — which is
; precisely what M3's third exit criterion ("gating on and off is chip-aligned")
; exists to catch. With one slot per two bits the alignment is a property of
; the data, not of the control flow, and there is nothing left to get wrong.
; The pad direction is in the same stream for the same reason: it switches on
; exactly the slot the data says, never a few cycles later.
;
; Autopull refills the OSR, so a starved FIFO stalls the pad in its last slot
; rather than emitting a half chip; every send ends in an explicit released
; slot so that last slot is high-impedance.
;
.program carrier_out
.wrap_target
    out pins, 1
    out pindirs, 1  [1]
.wrap

;
; fsk_out -- link v2's two-tone generator. link-v2-design.md S4, S7.
;
; One 32-bit word per chip, and the chip is a TONE rather than a gated square:
; there is no space, so the mark/space path-length objection above does not
; apply and the counted toggle loop the old comment rejects becomes available.
; A frame is 624 words = 2496 bytes, against the 78 kB a bit stream would need
; to carry two tones on one slot grid.
;
; The word is (periods-2) << 16 | half_loop, and the OSR shifts LEFT so the
; first out takes the top half. config.h derives both fields from the tone
; frequency and the system clock; nothing here is typed.
;
; CYCLE BUDGET, and it has to balance to the cycle.
;
;   half-period = set(1) + mov(1) + jmp x-- loop(isr+1)        = isr + 3
;   plus one delay cycle on the high set, or the jmp y-- makes
;   the low half one cycle longer                              = isr + 4
;   period                                                     = 2*isr + 8
;
; The [1] on the high set is NOT decoration. Without it the duty cycle is not
; 50 %, and energy lands in the EVEN harmonics -- the 2nd harmonic of 180 kHz
; is 360 kHz, which aliases at 500 ksps onto 140 kHz, which is guard bin 7.
; A contaminated guard is the v1 carrier floor wearing a new hat, and killing
; that is the whole point of the redesign. Each period must also be an EVEN
; number of cycles or the two halves cannot be equal; config.h asserts it.
;
; THE FIRST PERIOD IS WRITTEN OUT LONGHAND, and that is the one thing this
; program does that the brief did not plan for. The two out instructions cost
; two cycles, and in the obvious nine-instruction form they sit OUTSIDE the
; period loop -- so a chip comes to 45*800 + 2 = 36002 cycles, not 36000. Two
; cycles is 56 ppm of chip rate, it is the same sign every chip, and it
; therefore walks the chip clock down the frame: precisely the error the old
; comment above warns accumulates rather than scatters. Here the two outs are
; spent INSIDE the first period's own halves (its high half is
; set + out + mov + loop, its low half is set + mov + loop with no jmp y--),
; so that period costs 2*isr + 8 like every other one and a chip is exactly
; 36000 cycles = 250 us. The cost is six duplicated instructions.
;
; Consequence worth knowing at the bench: every HIGH half is exactly the tone
; half-period, and the slack lives in the low halves at the chip boundary
; (399 and 401 at tone A). Over a whole chip high and low are exactly equal,
; so the even harmonics are still null.
;
; A starved FIFO stalls at the first out with the pad driven LOW, not
; released -- there is no pindir in this stream (S4: constant envelope). The
; frame-level release is pio_carrier_drive()'s job and the caller's.
;
.program fsk_out
.wrap_target
    out y, 16                  ; periods - 2. Pad still low from the last chip.
    set pins, 1                ; the first period's high half starts here
    out isr, 16                ; half-period loop count
    mov x, isr
first_hi:
    jmp x--, first_hi
    set pins, 0                ; its low half: no jmp y--, so one cycle shorter
    mov x, isr
first_lo:
    jmp x--, first_lo
period:
    set pins, 1     [1]        ; [1] balances the jmp y-- in the low half
    mov x, isr
hi:
    jmp x--, hi
    set pins, 0
    mov x, isr
lo:
    jmp x--, lo
    jmp y--, period
.wrap

;
; edge_count — count rising edges on the same pad.
;
; x is preloaded with 0xFFFFFFFF by the CPU and decremented once per rising
; edge, so the count is 0xFFFFFFFF minus whatever x holds when the gate closes.
; Waiting for the low first means a pad that is already high when the gate
; opens cannot contribute a spurious first edge.
;
; Clocked at full system speed: at 200 kHz the pad changes every 375 cycles, so
; there is no question of missing one.
;
.program edge_count
count:
    wait 0 pin 0
    wait 1 pin 0
    jmp x--, count

;
; high_count -- how much of the time the pad is HIGH. An instrument.
;
; edge_count above answers "what frequency", which says nothing about the duty
; cycle, and the duty cycle is what decides whether energy lands in the EVEN
; harmonics -- one of which aliases onto a guard bin. Reading the guard bins
; through the amplifier cannot separate "the generator slipped" from "the
; amplifier distorted"; this can, because it is on the pad itself.
;
; Two cycles a pass, whatever the pin does, so the sampling is uniform:
;
;   pin low   jmp pin (not taken) + jmp loop      2 cycles, x unchanged
;   pin high  jmp pin (taken)     + jmp x--       2 cycles, x decremented
;
; So duty = decrements / (gate cycles / 2). x is preloaded all-ones by the CPU
; and the count is the difference, the same way edge_count works. At 144 MHz a
; 200 ms gate is 14.4 million passes, and 400 of them per period of tone A, so
; the sampling contributes nothing worth stating.
;
; jmp_pin must be set to the pad, and the pad's input buffer must be on.
;
.program high_count
loop:
    jmp pin, hi
    jmp loop
hi:
    jmp x--, loop
